for 20 days hundreds of indigenous  embera have been living in tents and  under plastic sheets in bogota's  national park located in the city center  like most here arnold and his family  were displaced from their village in  western colombia a year ago after being  caught in the crossfire of rising  violence between armed groups in the  military  at first they received housing  assistance and covet 19 subsidies but  the relief package ran out in september  and they've ended up here saying they  have nowhere else to go  we can't feed ourselves some eat some  don't and the winter is hard here if we  had a safe way to go back we would but  if we can't then we need help  the camp conditions are dire five kids  have ended up sick in intensive care  units this week  some 1 500 in beta are currently  homeless in the city few speak spanish a  thousand have been relocated to a camp  outside bogota with basic amenities city  authorities agree it's not ideal but  it's all they can offer  law is subsidies can't be given  indefinitely by the district they had an  expiration for the pandemic and the  victims law which is a national entity  should begin a reparations process we  are waiting for that to happen and  hopefully they will also be able to  return home  on friday the capitol police issued an  order of eviction giving authorities 48  hours to try and convince the embera to  voluntarily move to a different camp on  the outskirts of bogota before forcibly  removing them  a previous attempt this week to conduct  a pre-removal census ended in scuffles  2020 was the most violent year in more  than a decade in colombia amid  continuing conflict in remote areas many  more indigenous people could be forced  to resettle in cities like bogota in  coming months  thousands of indigenous people have had  to seek refuge in cities as a result of  the violence the war and the armed  groups operating in their territories  they are being sacked by those who want  to take the gold the wood and the other  resources all they want is to be able to  go back but unfortunately there is no  real return policy  a complicated humanitarian crisis that  critics say will only worsen unless its  root causes are addressed alessandra  piety al jazeera bogota
 

